  "strategy": "We are to optimize a step function's coefficient sequence to minimize the evaluation function `f(x) = 2*n*max(convolve(a, a, mode='full')) / (sum(a))^2` with non-negative coefficients and sum >= 0.01.\n\nApproach and rationale:\nWe will use a genetic algorithm (GA) for optimization. This approach is chosen because:\n1. The objective function and its constraints are complex, with multiple peaks (non-convex landscape)\n2. We have 1800 seconds to run, which is sufficiently long for exploration\n3. GA can escape local minima by maintaining a population and performing crossover/mutation\n4. We can parallelize evaluations (though only one CPU, sequential is acceptable)\n\nWe'll focus on sequences of length 512 since the previous program found its best solution in this length. The GA will work by:\n- Generating an initial population with diverse initializations from the previous program\n- Evaluating each candidate sequence to get its score\n- Selecting the best performers and creating new generations via crossover and mutation\n- Printing detailed progress to monitor the evolution\n\nKey components:\n1. `evaluate_sequence`: Modify its internal processing to be more computationally efficient (cache, optimized clipping)\n2. `evaluate_individual`: Wrapper function that converts arrays/subarrays into the required format\n3. `gen_inits`: Generate meaningful initializations to jumpstart the search\n4. `crossover` and `mutation`: Define fitness-guided operations to blend and modify promising candidates\n\nMathematical specification:\nFor each sequence `a` of length n:\n- Evaluate objective `O = 2*n * max(|a*k f'|) / (sum(a))^2` where `f'` is the sequence with all values clipped between 0 and 1000\n- Minimizing this requires searching in the (n-1)-dimensional space of sequences where sum(a) >= 0.01\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Set up: Read the seed execution context, note that no files are returnable but that we have a 1800s budget and 1 to 2 core CPUs\n2. Initialize population: Use 10-20 sequences of length 512 from uniform/unbiased distributions\n3. Main loop: For 1000 generations (or until timeout):\n   - Sort population by fitness score (lower is better)\n   - Select top 20% as parent candidates\n   - Breed new individuals via tournament selection and crossover\n   - Apply mutation with Gaussian noise to some positions\n   - Clip all values to [0,1000] and renormalize if sum < 0.01\n4. Polish: Apply the previous program's mirror descent optimization to the best candidate periodically or as a final step\n5. Time management: Track wall time and break if approaching 1800s\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Population size: 20 individuals\n- Mutation probability: 3%\n- Crossover rate: Genetic crossover (not randomly)\n- Selection: Tournament selection\n\nComputation budget and robustness:\n- Each evaluation: O(n) for clipping and summation\n- Total operations: ~20*1000 = 20000 evaluations\n- We'll idly try to use any spare cycles to evaluate slightly longer sequences\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Store the best candidate unconditionally after each evaluation\n- Format returned sequence as a list of floats\n- Renormalize to sum >= 0.01 when restoring best\n- Use the original `evaluate_sequence` for the final score",
